{"name":"Cupid’s Eye","hex":"#ff22dd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cupid","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A putto carrying a bow and arrow, representing Cupid or love.","Any of various lycaenid butterflies of the genera Chilades, Cupido and Everes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cupid"},{"term":"eye","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An organ through which animals see (“perceive surroundings via light”).","The visual sense."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To carefully or appraisingly observe (someone or something).","To consider."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eye"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eye","blocks":[{"text":"An eye is a sensory organ that allows an organism to perceive visual information. It detects light and converts it into electro-chemical impulses in neurons (neurones). It is part of an organism's visual system."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Krilleyekils.jpg/330px-Krilleyekils.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye","wikidata":"Q7364"},{"title":"Cupid","blocks":[{"text":"In classical mythology, Cupid is the god of desire, erotic love, attraction and affection. He is often portrayed as the son of the love goddess Venus and the god of war Mars. He is also known as Amor. His Greek counterpart is Eros.\nAlthough Eros is generally portrayed as a slender winged youth in Classical Greek art, during the Hellenistic period, he was increasingly portrayed as a chubby boy. During this time, his iconography acquired the bow and arrow that represent his source of power: a person, or even a deity, who is shot by Cupid's arrow is filled with uncontrollable desire. In myths, Cupid is a minor character who serves mostly to set the plot in motion. He is a main character only in the tale of Cupid and Psyche, in which he is wounded by his own weapons and experiences the ordeal of love. Although other extended stories are not told about him, his tradition is rich in poetic themes and visual scenarios, such as \"Love conquers all\" and the retaliatory punishment or torture of Cupid."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Bertel_Thorvaldsen_-_Cupid.jpg/330px-Bertel_Thorvaldsen_-_Cupid.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cupid","wikidata":"Q5011"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Bison_bonasus_right_eye_close-up.jpg/500px-Bison_bonasus_right_eye_close-up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Bison_bonasus_right_eye_close-up.jpg/500px-Bison_bonasus_right_eye_close-up.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Eye of a European bison","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABison_bonasus_right_eye_close-up.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/%D0%93%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B7.jpg/500px-%D0%93%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/%D0%93%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B7.jpg/1280px-%D0%93%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/%D0%93%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B7.jpg/500px-%D0%93%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Human eye","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%D0%93%D0%BE%D0%BB%D1%83%D0%B1%D